 ## Overview
-Second-Order Chamfer Distance is a supervised geometric loss function that measures the discrepancy between a predicted mesh vertex and a ground truth surface by incorporating local surface curvature [S1]. Unlike standard Chamfer Distance, which uses a simple quadratic point-to-point distance, this method approximates the distance function quadratically to account for the local geometry of the reference surface [S1]. It serves as a baseline for meshing implicit functions where ground truth meshes are available [S1].
+Second-Order Chamfer Distance is a supervised geometric loss function that measures the discrepancy between a predicted mesh vertex and a ground truth surface by incorporating local surface curvature [S1]. Unlike standard Chamfer Distance, which uses a simple quadratic point-to-point distance, this method approximates the distance function quadratically to account for the local geometry of the reference surface [S1]. It serves as a supervised baseline for meshing an implicit function when the ground truth mesh is explicitly available [S1].
 
